jwe — provenance note
======================

Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement precedent as the sibling
`tlsresume`/`dtls` modules — self-contained crypto-adjacent modules not
folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

jwe — clean-room implementation from RFC 7516 (JSON Web Encryption), RFC
7518 (JSON Web Algorithms — the `alg`/`enc` algorithm definitions), and RFC
3394 (AES Key Wrap, the `aeskw.zig` core). These are open,
public IETF/informational specifications — not copyrightable works (merger
doctrine; see CONVENTIONS.md §5) — so no third-party-source NOTICE entry is
strictly required for the RFC citations alone.

This module names one real design/code reference: `modules/dnp3/src/sa.zig`
(this repo's own DNP3 Secure Authentication module), whose `aeskw` namespace
already implements RFC 3394 AES Key Wrap (AES-128/256 KEK) and is byte-exact
against the RFC 3394 §4 published vectors. `jwe`'s own `aeskw.zig` is
**mirrored from that reference** (re-expressed against the RFC text, then
independently validated against the same §4.1 vector) and does NOT import
`dnp3` — sibling modules stay independent per CONVENTIONS.md's
naming/structure rules; no code needed license care since both are this
repo's own MIT-licensed work.

`jwe` also names its sibling `jwt` (RFC 7515 JWS/RFC 7519 JWT) as the design
precedent for this module's JOSE-header/base64url/error-handling house
style (compact-serialization parsing shape, arena-per-call ownership,
`fromString`-into-`.unknown` enum mapping) — again this repo's own work, no
third-party source involved.

`std.crypto.aead.aes_gcm`, `std.crypto.pwhash.pbkdf2`,
`std.crypto.auth.hmac`, `std.json`, and `std.base64` are used via their
public std APIs (MIT, part of Zig itself). The `rsa` module (this repo's
own, RFC 8017 clean-room — see its own NOTICE/SPEC) supplies the RSA-OAEP
primitives via its public `encryptOaep`/`decryptOaep`.

No GPL/LGPL/AGPL source was consulted or copied anywhere in this module.

**Status: real — no stubs remain.** `dir`, RSA-OAEP/RSA-OAEP-256 (wraps
`rsa`), ECDH-ES/ECDH-ES+A128KW/ECDH-ES+A256KW (RFC 7518 §4.6,
ephemeral-static ECDH on P-256 — via the `p256` module — or X25519, feeding
the Concat KDF; byte-exact against RFC 7518 Appendix C), A128GCM/A256GCM
content encryption, A128CBC-HS256/A256CBC-HS512 content encryption (RFC
7518 §5.2, byte-exact against Appendix B.1/B.3), A128GCMKW/A256GCMKW key
wrap, A128KW/A256KW + PBES2-* key wrap (RFC 3394, byte-exact against §4.1
and RFC 7516 A.3 both directions), the JOSE header codec, and the
compact-serialization encrypt/decrypt dispatch — all tested (KATs where the
assigned RFCs publish them; RFC 7516 A.1 decrypt-direction byte-exact). The
only remaining limitation is AES-192 (`A192GCM`/`A192GCMKW`/`A192KW`/
`A192CBC-HS384`/`ECDH-ES+A192KW`): Zig 0.16's std ships no AES-192 cipher at
all — a typed `error.UnsupportedKeyLength`, documented in SPEC.md (the B.2
vector's HMAC half is still validated in tests).
